Sodium ion batteries (SIBs) have been regarded as one of the promising candidates for medium- or large-scale energy storage owing to their abundant and cost-effective resources. $^{1,2}$ With the growing demand of electric vehicles and portable electronic devices in regions characterized by dramatic temperature and climate differences, improving the tolerance of SIBs at both elevated and subzero temperatures is crucial for their widespread application. $^{3}$ However, current SIBs are still plagued by severe parasitic reactions at high temperature (HT) and sluggish ionic transfer kinetics at low temperature (LT). $^{3,4}

